<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Herod the Great</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 73–4 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Herod the Great was an Idumaean named king of Judea by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a> in 40 BCE, who took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> from the last Hasmonean king in 37 BCE. A Roman client, he rebuilt the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Temple in Jerusalem</a> and built Caesarea Maritima and fortresses such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masada">Masada</a>. After his death his kingdom was divided among his sons.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herod-the-great.jpg" alt="Herod the Great" width="320" height="294" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HerodtheGreat2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Herod was born in Idumea, south of Judea, the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipater">Antipater</a> the Idumaean, a high-ranking official under the ethnarch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyrcanus-ii">Hyrcanus II</a>, and Cypros, a Nabatean princess from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petra">Petra</a>. His father was by descent an Edomite, and his ancestors had been forcibly converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>. Herod was raised as a Jew. Historians dispute his ethnic background: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> held that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idumaeans">Idumaeans</a> were of Nabataean origin, while others argue that the Edomites were not Arab. Josephus, whose works record many details of his life, made him a descendant of the Hasmoneans.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Judea had been ruled by Hasmonean kings from 140 until 63 BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>'s conquest made them clients of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Herod's war against the Hasmonean Antigonus began in 39 BCE, and after the conquest of Jerusalem Mark Antony executed Antigonus. Herod ruled under Roman overlordship until his death and established the Herodian dynasty. In 30 BCE Octavian confirmed him as King of Judea at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>.</p><p>His most ambitious project was the expansion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, begun in 20-19 BCE, for which he employed 1,000 priests as masons and carpenters to comply with religious law. It was destroyed in 70 CE, and only the retaining walls remain, including the Western Wall. He also built the fortresses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masada">Masada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodium">Herodium</a> and founded Caesarea Maritima. Late in his reign he had his sons Alexander and Aristobulus tried before a Roman court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>, and they were executed. The Gospel of Matthew describes him ordering the killing of all boys of two and under in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a>, the Massacre of the Innocents.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Herod died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jericho">Jericho</a> after a painful illness. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> says the pain led him to attempt suicide by stabbing, and that his cousin stopped him. Most 20th-century scholarship followed Emil Schürer's chronology of 1896 in dating his death to 4 BCE. Josephus says he was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodium">Herodium</a>, and in 2007 a team led by Ehud Netzer announced the discovery of a tomb there, with a broken sarcophagus but no remains.</p><p>Augustus respected Herod's will and divided the kingdom among three sons. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-archelaus">Herod Archelaus</a> became ethnarch of Judea, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a> and Idumea, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-antipas">Herod Antipas</a> tetrarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peraea">Peraea</a>, and Philip tetrarch of the lands north and east of the Jordan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Herod the Great” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Antipater the Idumaean, progenitor of the Herodian dynasty, was of Idumean origin, and so was <b>Herod the Great</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/edom">Edom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Reckoning from the death of <b>Herod</b> would place the birth between 6 and 4 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Herod the Great's</b> will named Archelaus as heir to the kingship, but Augustus in 4 BCE gave him only the title ethnarch and the heartland of the kingdom: Judea with Jerusalem, Samaria and Idumaea, about half of his father's territory, with the promise of a crown if he…</blockquote><a href="/state/herodian-ethnarchy-of-judea">Herodian Ethnarchy of Judea</a></li><li><blockquote>Herod Agrippa I, grandson of <b>Herod the Great</b> and friend of Caligula, received the former Tetrarchy of Philip with the title king in 37.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-herod-agrippa">Kingdom of Herod Agrippa</a></li><li><blockquote>After the death of <b>Herod the Great</b> in 4 BCE, Augustus gave his son Herod Antipas two separate territories: Galilee, west of the Sea of Galilee, and Peraea, a strip east of the Jordan.</blockquote><a href="/state/tetrarchy-of-galilee-and-peraea">Tetrarchy of Galilee and Peraea</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>Herod the Great</b> died in 4 BCE, Augustus confirmed his will with changes and divided the kingdom among three sons.</blockquote><a href="/state/tetrarchy-of-philip">Tetrarchy of Philip</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Edom. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jericho">Jericho</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.86,35.46&amp;year=-29" aria-label="Jericho on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5463 1004 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Herod the Great"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5913 1274h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5913 1274h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=31.86,35.46&amp;year=-29">What was here in 29 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/mariamne-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mariamne-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Mariamne I</span><span class="tp">Married &#183; d. 29 BCE</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/herod-archelaus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/herod-archelaus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Herod Archelaus</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; 23 BCE – c. 18 CE</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/herod-antipas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/herod-antipas.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Herod Antipas</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; c. 20 BCE–after 39 CE</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Go and search diligently for the young child; and when ye have found him, bring me word again, that I may come and worship him also.&#8221;</p><footer>Matthew 2:8, to the wise men from the east &#183; King James Version, 1611 &#183; as Matthew's Gospel tells it &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_(King_James)/Matthew" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
